# OVERDUE — THE DRIVE, the way out to GOODCO.
#
# The road's outbound score, and the older half of a PAIR: AN HOUR BEHIND
# (`content/songs/hour_behind.song`) is the same tarmac driven the other way,
# after she has left the planet. This one is a PANIC rather than a chase — the
# jacket has been answering from the campus for an hour and he is still in
# traffic.
#
# ── IT IS A FIFTY-SECOND PIECE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REWRITE ───────────────
#
# A LEG IS NOT A LEVEL. Every other score in this game is a bed under something
# that runs for minutes: two minutes of loop is the right length because nobody
# hears the seam. A leg of the road is over in forty to sixty seconds — a
# skilled driver covers the 25.6k px course at an average that lands there, and
# the auto-driver's cautious hundred-and-twenty is the slow end, not the norm.
#
# So the ten-section, seventy-six-bar version of this score was a track whose
# BREAK, BUILD and TURN nobody had ever heard. The player got the intro, the
# anthem, the chorus and a restatement, and then the car arrived and the music
# was cut off in the middle of a breakdown. Forty-five percent of the writing
# was for a listener who does not exist.
#
# This is thirty-six bars — 53 s — so the arc LANDS inside the drive: the card
# lifts on the clock, the anthem gets going, it changes gear, the chorus, gear
# again, and the turn peaks about where the town thins out. A cautious driver
# hears it twice, which is what a loop is for. Nobody hears half of it.
#
# THREE DECISIONS, unchanged, because they are what this track IS.
#
#   A MODE THAT REFUSES TO SETTLE — a Phrygian i–♭II lurch, Dm → E♭, up a
#   semitone and back, the one move in the piece that never resolves.
#
#   ONE INTERVAL AS A SIGNATURE — a two-tone sawtooth SIREN wailing a minor
#   second, A against A♯, which is a chord tone in BOTH of those chords. The
#   emergency signal and the harmony are the same thing, so it never sounds
#   pasted on.
#
#   THE CLOCK NEVER STOPS — a dry quarter-note tick in every single pattern.
#   He was late before he got in the car, and the one sound the arrangement
#   never takes away is the one counting the time he does not have. (Which is
#   exactly what the homeward score DELETES, because by then there is nothing
#   to be late for.)
#
# TWO THINGS THE SHORT FORM CHANGED, and both are general.
#
#   THE BUILD IS NOT A SECTION, IT IS A GEAR CHANGE. Eight bars of chromatic
#   climb once a loop is a fifth of a fifty-second piece spent going up. CLIMB
#   is FOUR bars, twice as steep — two chords a bar, D–D♯–E–F–F♯–G–G♯–A in
#   bare parallel fifths — and it happens TWICE, once into the chorus and once
#   into the turn. A hinge rather than a destination, which is also the more
#   honest thing for a car to be doing.
#
#   THE INTRO DOES THE BREAKDOWN'S JOB. There is no BREAK any more; fifty
#   seconds has no room to go quiet in the middle. What the breakdown was for —
#   the clock alone, with the arrangement stripped off it — is the first bar of
#   the piece, which is also the bar the title card sits on. The thin moment
#   moved to the only place a short score can afford one.
#
# The signature is placed for a listener who gets ONE pass: the siren arrives in
# bar 5 of A rather than waiting for a restatement, sits out the chorus so its
# return has somewhere to come back from, and tightens across each CLIMB from
# half notes to sixteenths.
#
# Compile:  node scripts/song-import.mjs content/songs/overdue.song --sheet

id     overdue
title  OVERDUE
tempo  162
about
  The road's theme, and it is a panic rather than a chase — written short, to
  the length of a leg, so the whole arc lands before the car does. A D-minor
  drive with a Phrygian i–♭II lurch (Dm → E♭) that never settles, a galloping
  triangle bass under a chugging saw engine an octave above it, and a two-tone
  sawtooth SIREN wailing a minor second over the top — the same interval an
  emergency vehicle uses, and here it is a chord tone in both chords, so the
  distress signal is also the harmony. A dry clock ticks every quarter from the
  first bar to the last, and the first bar is the clock on its own: there is no
  breakdown in a fifty-second piece, so the intro is where it goes thin. Twice
  the arrangement changes gear on a four-bar chromatic climb in bare fifths,
  D up to A, the siren tightening across it — once into the chorus, once into
  the turn. Shapes borrowed from the action scores of the era, every melody
  original.

voice siren    sawtooth vol=.021 gate=.95 detune=9 echo=.26 vib=5.5/22/60
voice lead     square vol=.028 gate=.8 detune=6 echo=.2 vib=6.5/12/100
voice harm     square vol=.014 gate=.8 pan=.35 echo=.16
voice stab     square vol=.013 gate=.25 pan=-0.32
# THE ENGINE — sixteenths on the root, an OCTAVE ABOVE the bass. See the header.
voice pulse    sawtooth vol=.017 gate=.5 pan=.22 lp=1200
voice bass     triangle vol=.056 gate=.85

drum  tick     noise vol=.009 gate=.12 pan=-0.45 bp=3200
drum  kick     triangle vol=.064 gate=1 slide=.25 note=D2
drum  snare    noise vol=.036 gate=.75 hp=1500
drum  hat      noise vol=.01 gate=.25 pan=.25 hp=6800

# ── INTRO — the clock alone, then the engine, then the first wail. This is the
#    bar and a half the title card sits on, and it is also this score's whole
#    supply of quiet: the long version's breakdown lived here now ────────────
section intro
  chords Dm | Dm | Dm | Dm
  siren  .*16 | .*16 | .*16 | a4*8 a#4*8
  pulse  .*16 | .*16 | chug:4 | chug:4
  bass   .*16 | gallop:2 | gallop:2 | gallop:2
  kick   ................|................|x.....x.x.......|x.....x.x.x.....
  hat    ................|................|x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.|x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.
  tick   x...x...x...x...

# ── A — the anthem. The siren joins at the halfway line rather than waiting
#    for a restatement that this arrangement no longer has ───────────────────
section a
  chords Dm | Eb | Dm | Eb | Dm | Bb | C | A
  lead   d5*2 .*2 d5*2 .*2 f5*4 e5*4 | d#5*2 .*2 d#5*2 .*2 d5*4 c5*4 | d5*2 .*2 f5*2 .*2 a5*4 g5*2 f5*2 | d#5*4 d5*4 c5*4 a4*4
         d5*2 .*2 d5*2 .*2 f5*2 e5*2 d5*2 c5*2 | a#4*4 d5*4 f5*4 d5*4 | c5*4 e5*4 g5*4 e5*2 c5*2 | a4*2 c#5*2 e5*4 c#5*2 a4*2 .*4
  siren  .*16 | .*16 | .*16 | .*16
         a5*8 a5*8 | a#5*8 a#5*8 | a5*8 a5*8 | a#5*8 .*8
  stab   offbeat 4
  pulse  chug 4
  bass   gallop 2
  kick   x.....x.x.......|x.....x.x.x.....
  snare  ....x.......x...
  hat    x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.
  tick   x...x...x...x...

# ── CLIMB — the gear change. Two chords a bar, D up to A in bare fifths, the
#    siren tightening from half notes to sixteenths across the four. Played
#    twice a loop, which is what makes it a hinge and not a finale ───────────
section climb
  chords Dm D#m | Em Fm | F#m Gm | G#m A
  lead   d5*2 a5*2 d5*2 a5*2 d#5*2 a#5*2 d#5*2 a#5*2 | e5*2 b5*2 e5*2 b5*2 f5*2 c6*2 f5*2 c6*2
         f#5*2 c#6*2 f#5*2 c#6*2 g5*2 d6*2 g5*2 d6*2 | g#5*2 d#6*2 g#5*2 d#6*2 a5*2 e6*2 a5*2 e6*2
  siren  a5*8 a#5*8 | a5*4 a#5*4 a5*4 a#5*4
         a5*2 a#5*2 a5*2 a#5*2 a5*2 a#5*2 a5*2 a#5*2 | a5 a#5 a5 a#5 a5 a#5 a5 a#5 a5 a#5 a5 a#5 a5 a#5 a5 a#5
  stab   offbeat 4
  pulse  chug 4
  bass   drive 2
  kick   x.......x.......|x.......x.......|x.......x...x...|x.......x...x...
  snare  ....x.......x...|....x.......x.x.|....x...x.x.x.x.|x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.
  hat    xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
  tick   x...x...x...x...

# ── B — the chorus, and the one section with no siren in it. It has to be
#    missing from somewhere or its return in the second CLIMB is just more ───
section b
  chords Bb | C | Dm | Dm | Bb | C | Gm | A
  lead   d5*4 f5*4 a#5*8 | c5*4 e5*4 g5*8 | a5*6 g5*2 f5*4 d5*4 | e5*4 f5*4 a5*8
         a#5*4 a5*2 g5*2 f5*4 d5*4 | c5*4 e5*2 g5*2 a#5*4 g5*4 | g5*4 a#5*4 d6*4 a#5*2 g5*2 | a5*8 e5*4 c#5*4
  harm   d4*4 f4*4 a#4*8 | c4*4 e4*4 g4*8 | a4*6 g4*2 f4*4 d4*4 | e4*4 f4*4 a4*8
         a#4*4 a4*2 g4*2 f4*4 d4*4 | c4*4 e4*2 g4*2 a#4*4 g4*4 | g4*4 a#4*4 d5*4 a#4*2 g4*2 | a4*8 e4*4 c#4*4
  stab   offbeat 4
  pulse  chug 4
  bass   gallop 2
  kick   x.....x.x.......|x.....x.x.x.....
  snare  ....x.......x...
  hat    x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.
  tick   x...x...x...x...

# ── TURN — the peak, and roughly where the town thins out ──────────────────
section turn
  chords Dm | Bb | C | A | Dm | Bb | A | A
  lead   d5*2 e5*2 f5*2 g5*2 a5*8 | a#5*2 a5*2 g5*2 f5*2 d5*8 | c5*2 e5*2 g5*2 a5*2 a#5*4 a5*2 g5*2 | e5*2 c#5*2 a4*4 c#5*2 e5*2 a5*4
         d5*2 f5*2 a5*4 d6*4 a5*2 f5*2 | a#5*4 a5*2 g5*2 f5*4 d5*4 | e5*4 g5*4 a5*4 c#6*4 | d6*8 .*8
  harm   d4*2 e4*2 f4*2 g4*2 a4*8 | a#4*2 a4*2 g4*2 f4*2 d4*8 | c4*2 e4*2 g4*2 a4*2 a#4*4 a4*2 g4*2 | e4*2 c#4*2 a3*4 c#4*2 e4*2 a4*4
         d4*2 f4*2 a4*4 d5*4 a4*2 f4*2 | a#4*4 a4*2 g4*2 f4*4 d4*4 | e4*4 g4*4 a4*4 c#5*4 | d5*8 .*8
  siren  .*16 | .*16 | .*16 | .*16
         a5*8 a5*8 | a#5*8 a#5*8 | a5*8 a#5*8 | d6*8 .*8
  stab   offbeat 4
  pulse  chug 4
  bass   gallop 2
  kick   x.....x.x.......|x.....x.x.x.....
  snare  ....x.......x...|....x.......x...|....x.......x...|....x.......x...
         ....x.......x...|....x.......x...|....x.......x.x.|x.x.xx.xxxxx....
  hat    x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.
  tick   x...x...x...x...

order  intro a climb b climb turn
